### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

Follow up to #13103, caught by AI trying to implement / add tests, cc @annevk 

When a top-level navigable navigates to about:blank, [create and initialize a Document object](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/document-lifecycle.html#initialise-the-document-object) step 7.7 says:

> 7. Let topLevelCreationURL be creationURL.

which is about:blank. The [secure context](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#secure-context) algorithm then hands that to [Is url potentially trustworthy?](https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-secure-contexts/#potentially-trustworthy-url):

> 1. If url is “about:blank” or “about:srcdoc”, return “Potentially Trustworthy”.

That step assumes the document “inherits context from its creator”, but at the top level nothing is inherited, so the document is a secure context whatever its origin. An http: page can open a same-origin popup, set w.location = "about:blank", and use [SecureContext] APIs through w.

Example

Served from http://example.com/:
```html
<script>
const w = window.open("/page.html");
w.onload = () => {
    w.location = "about:blank";
    setTimeout(() => {
        // w's document has origin http://example.com and top-level creation URL about:blank.
        console.log(w.isSecureContext); // spec: true. Expected: false.
    }, 500);
};
</script>
```

(Implementations do not match the spec and behave as expected)
